\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan ends firm,exporters sell more dollars. \b0\par\par\uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2000\par 06:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Nov 1 (Reuters) - China's yuan rose further against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday, boosted by solid selling of the American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by domestic exporters. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2772 to one dollar, compared with 8.2775 on Tuesday, after creeping in a tight intraday range of 8.2770 and 8.2778. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yuan turnover eased to a moderate $200 million from Tuesday's $250 million, according to figures published on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn}}}\uc2 , China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System's website. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar supply remained pretty strong today. It's very likely banks have bought more dollars from their clients recently," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Most Chinese companies are required to sell their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings to banks, which in turn settle \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Since early October, the yuan has reversed its earlier weakness and steered towards the strong end of its long-standing trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the firming of the yuan, convertible only on the current account, could have been helped by a likely pick-up in China's exports. \par \par \uc2  The strengthening in the yuan has gained pace since last week and some dealers said this was probably because many domestic exporters like to wrap up their dollar holdings by the end of the month for book closing purposes. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said they expected the yuan to retrace this month as the bout of solid dollar supply might be offset by higher imports. But others said the yuan might test the 8.2770 level this week. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to block the yuan from closing outside the band on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of China has sandwiched the yuan within the hair-thin trading band for nearly three years through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Officials say \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to move towards a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime eventually, partly to counter the impact on trade of accession to the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  But they are convinced the full convertibility of the yuan could be years away. \par \par \uc2  Yi Gang, deputy general secretary of the monetary policy committee of the People's Bank of China said the yuan could be fully convertible by 2015. \par \par \uc2  "Considering mainland China's earlier realisation of convertibility on the current account and this process of proceeding systematically, the renminbi (yuan) might be fully convertible on the capital account in 2015," Yi said in the text of a speech seen on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The remark had no impact on the tightly controlled market. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the yuan lost ground against the yen to close at 7.6050 versus Tuesday's 7.4998. It ended unchanged at 1.0610 to the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwb102veb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
