\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan ends up on exporter dollar selling. \b0\par\par\uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2000\par 06:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Oct 30 (Reuters) - China's yuan ended firmer against the U.S. dollar on Monday, buoyed by dollar selling from local banks on behalf of exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2778 to one dollar, compared with 8.2785 on Friday after moving in a narrow range of 8.2777 and 8.2789. \par \par \uc2  Turnover rose slightly to $150 million from Friday's $140 million, according to figures published on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn}}}\uc2 , China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System's website. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has firmed towards the strong end of its normal thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 over the past three weeks. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar selling from domestic banks on behalf of exporters is a key factor pushing up the yuan recently," said a local bank dealer. "This signals exports have picked up in the past few weeks." \par \par \uc2  Chinese trade firms are required to settle most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings with local banks designated to trade on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Trade is key to dollar supply and demand on the market as China's yuan is only convertible on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to enforce the 8.2770 to 8.2800 band, partly to deter speculation of a possible yuan devaluation. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had reduced its activity in the market since mid-April, allowing the yuan to close outside its thin trading band a dozen times. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Finance Minister Xiang Huaicheng and U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said in a statement after a China-U.S. Joint Economic Committee meeting in Washington that China needs a more flexible exchange rate to boost its economic development and integrate with the world economy. \par \par \uc2  But Xiang also told a news conference it was up to the market, not the government, to determine the right time for making the yuan more easily exchangable. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Beijing was expected to move towards a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime eventually, partly to counter challenges from China's entry into the World Trade Organisation, but a move was unlikely until at least end-2001. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was likely to lose steam after its gains in the past few weeks and was expected to move narrowly around the 8.2780 level in the near term. \par \par \uc2  The yuan weakened against the Japanese yen to end at 7.6295 to 100 yen from 7.4863 on Friday. It ended little changed against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0607 from 1.0606. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwau030f1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
