\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan down as exporter dollar selling eases. \b0\par\par\uc2 336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2000\par 06:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Oct 26 (Reuters) - China's yuan weakened against the U.S. dollar on Thursday as dollar selling by local banks on behalf of exporters eased, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2790 to one dollar, compared with 8.2779 on Wednesday. It moved in a range of 8.2780 and 8.2805. \par \par \uc2  Turnover rose to $170 million from Wednesday's $130 million, according to figures published on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn}}}\uc2 , the website of the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has firmed towards the strong end of its normal thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 over the past few weeks, buoyed by increased dollar settlement from local exporters. \par \par \uc2  Chinese trade firms are required to settle most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings with local banks designated to trade on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar selling eased today, giving an opportunity for the hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rebound," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Despite a brief breach of its normal trading band, there were no signs that the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the yuan, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to enforce the band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 for nearly three years, although dealers said it has reduced its activity on the market in the past few months. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has closed outside its band more than a dozen times since mid-April. \par \par \uc2  Officials say China is considering a broader trading band for the yuan, but analysts predict a widening was unlikely until late next year. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was likely to move narrowly around current levels in the near term. \par \par \uc2  The yuan strengthened slightly against the Japanese yen to end at 7.6354 to 100 yen from 7.6577 on Wednesday. It ended up solidly at 1.0506 against the Hong Kong dollar from Wednesday's 1.0614 due to a last-minute speculative trading, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwaq03fga\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
