\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan marginally weakens in active trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 371 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2000\par 06:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Oct 20 (Reuters) - China's yuan ended one notch softer against the U.S. dollar after a session of zigzag trade. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2780 to one dollar, compared with 8.2779 on Thursday, after creeping in an intraday range between 8.2776 and 8.2782. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yuan turnover, available on the trading centre's website, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn}}}\uc2 , expanded to $290 million from a moderate $190 million on Thursday. Turnover over the week was $1.07 billion. \par \par \uc2  "The market was pretty active today and dollar supply still remained relatively strong in early trade," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "But some large dollar buying orders came in late trading and pushed down the yuan," he said. \par \par \uc2  Chinese banks usually settle their \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions on the national market after trading the yuan with their corporate clients, mostly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Since a week-long National Day holiday ended on October 7, the yuan has headed for the firmer end of its long-standing band of 8.2770 and 8.2800, which the central People's Bank of China has enforced for nearly three years. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trade surplus could contract during the fourth quarter of the year because imports might continue to outpace exports. \par \par \uc2  China had a trade surplus of $19.2 billion in the first nine months of this year, compared with $19.39 billion the same period of last year, according to official statistics. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the declining trade surplus could put pressure on the yuan, though they did not expect Beijing would devalue it any time soon. \par \par \uc2  State media said on Thursday China would maintain a stable yuan and improve its managed floating \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime based on market demand and supply during 2001 to 2005. \par \par \uc2  The media reports quoted the 10th Five-Year Plan (2001-2005) adopted by the Communist Party's central committee. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yuan gained ground against the yen to end at 7.6264, compared with 7.6760 to 100 yen on Thursday. It inched down to close at 1.0610 versus one Hong Kong dollar from 1.0609. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwak03u4a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
