\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan rises as importers buy fewer dollars. \b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2000\par 07:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Oct 12 (Reuters) - China's yuan firmed against the U.S. dollar on Thursday as buying of the hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by domestic importers eased. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2784 to one dollar, one notch below its intraday high of 8.2783 after a session of steady climbing. It closed at 8.2795 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar buying dropped a lot from the level yesterday," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Chinese banks usually settle their \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions on the national market after trading the yuan with their corporate clients, mostly importers and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Late buying of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by domestic importers pushed the yuan down on Wednesday to an intraday low of 8.2800, a key psychological level which the central People's Bank of China has defended for nearly three years. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yuan from closing outside the weaker end of the long-standing band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 as renewed rumours of a yuan devaluation hit some regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early trade. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said, however, there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dai Xianglong, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, said on Tuesday the government would maintain the "basic stability" of the yuan, a slight difference from earlier pledges of "stability". \par \par \uc2  The comment stirred speculation of a possible shift in yuan policy. But in response to rumours that a devaluation was under consideration, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman said on Wednesday that China's yuan policy remained unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they were convinced a devaluation was not on Beijing's reform agenda because the impact of membership of the World Trade Organisation on trade remained unclear. \par \par \uc2  The yuan, convertible only on the current account, is driven mainly by China's imports and exports. \par \par \uc2  They said the government would continue to defend the 8.2800 level, at least this year. \par \par \uc2  The market turnover for Thursday was not immediately available on the trading centre's website, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn}}}\uc2 . Dollar/yuan turnover was $250 million on Wednesday, sharply higher than $190 million on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the yuan strengthened against the yen to 7.6353 to 100 yen, compared with 7.6602 on Wednesday. It weakened against the Hong Kong dollar to close two notches down at 1.063. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwac04dbc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
