\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  yuan ends weaker, c. bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  seen. \b0\par\par\uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2000\par 07:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Oct 11 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan lost ground against the dollar on Wednesday, pushed down by late buying of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by domestic importers. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2795 to one dollar, compared with 8.2787 on Tuesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved to support the yuan as it hit the low end of its long-standing band, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan struck an intraday low of 8.2800, a key psychological level, minutes before the market close and dealers said the central People's Bank of China was seen preventing the yuan from closing outside the weak end of its range. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan went straight towards the 8.2800 level in late trade as more banks were buying dollars," said one local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "But strong orders supported the yuan from falling further, and it was like what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually does," he said. \par \par \uc2  Chinese banks usually settle their \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions on the national market after trading the yuan with their corporate clients. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which helped box the yuan in a wafer-thin rage of 8.2770 and 8.2800 for nearly three years, reappeared on Wednesday when renewed rumours of a yuan devaluation battered some \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht touched a new 27-month low in early Asian trade following sustained weakness in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and market speculation that China might devalue the yuan, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rumours surfaced after Dai Xianglong, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, said on Tuesday that Beijing would maintain the "basic stability" of the yuan, a slight difference from earlier pledges of "stability". \par \par \uc2  In response to rumours that Beijing was considering a devaluation, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman said on Wednesday that China's yuan policy remained unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Brokers were convinced a move to devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not on Beijing's reform agenda, even though the monetary policy committee used the words "basically stable" as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  management target. \par \par \uc2  "Beijing might be just setting a tone for the long-term direction as it has promised market reforms again and again," said a dealer at an European bank in Shanghai. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong said in September the government would consider widening the trading band of the yuan if necessary, but changes would not be major. He gave no timetable. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think there would be an immediate swing in the yuan trading band because there are other changes happening and the government may have to dispel uncertainty before freeing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a bit," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the government would continue to defend the 8.2800 level until the impact on foreign trade performance of membership of the World Trade Organisation became clear. \par \par \uc2  The yuan, only convertible under the current account, is mainly driven by China's imports and exports. \par \par \uc2  The government also has pledged to reform its strict interest rate regime, which would affect the financial sector, including the yuan, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think Beijing will move forward with all changes at the same time. It's much riskier than moving one step after another," said an analyst at a domestic research house who declined to be identified. \par \par \uc2  Some speculators may be also trying to capitalise on China's recent release of daily \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  turnover figures. \par \par \uc2  The market turnover for Wednesday was not immediately available on the trading centre's website, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn}}}\uc2 . Dollar/yuan turnover was $190 million on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the yuan ended softer against the yen at 7.6602, compared with 7.6200 to 100 yen on Tuesday. It also weakened against the Hong Kong dollar to close at 1.0611 versus 1.0602. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwab04jm0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
