\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan ends down in thin trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2000\par 06:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Oct 8 (Reuters) - China's yuan weakened against the U.S. dollar in extremely thin trade on Sunday, the first trading day after a long holiday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2795 to one dollar, compared with 8.2788 on September 30 after moving in a range of 8.2789 and 8.2844. \par \par \uc2  The Shanghai-based national market, the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System, was closed from October 1-7 for China's National Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  "The market was still in a holiday mood today with only a handful of transactions being done," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan is likely to move narrowly in thin trade in the next two or three days with the market recovering from the holiday mood," he said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan traded near the soft end of its normal thin trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 in the last week before the holiday as Chinese banks bought dollars for local importers, an indication of strong imports. \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan in the thin band over the past three years, partly to quell speculation about a yuan devaluation. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not on the market on Sunday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Chinese trading companies have to convert most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings into yuan using local banks designated to trade on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Trade is key to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply on the market because the yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan's trend in the medium term after the holiday would still depend on China's foreign trade performance. \par \par \uc2  On Sunday, the yuan strengthened against the Japanese yen at 7.5405 to 100 yen from 7.7300 on September 30. It ended a touch higher at 1.0608 to the Hong Kong dollar from 1.0610. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwa805ahr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
