\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends stronger before holiday. \b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2000\par 06:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     SHANGHAI, Sept 30 (Reuters) - China's yuan\tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par firmed on Saturday in light volume on the last trading session\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  ahead the week-long National Holiday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2      The yuan ended at 8.2788 to the U.S. dollar, up from 8.2791 \par \par \uc2  on Friday, after moving in a range between 8.2783 and 8.2825. \par \par \uc2      The Shanghai-based national market will close from October \par \par \uc2  1-7 and will resume trading on October 8. \par \par \uc2      Most deals had been done during the week and there were few \par \par \uc2  transactions on Saturday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2      "It's extremely quiet today because everybody is in the \par \par \uc2  holiday mood," said a dealer at a foreign bank. "Most \par \par \uc2  transactions had already been done yesterday and some people \par \par \uc2  are waiting until after the holidays." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected the yuan to move in a narrow \par \par \uc2  range of 8.2790 and 8.2800 after the break, the weaker side of \par \par \uc2  its thin long-standing trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has traded near the soft end of its trading band \par \par \uc2  this week as Chinese banks bought dollars for local importers, \par \par \uc2  an indication of strong imports. \par \par \uc2  Chinese trading firms have to convert most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2  exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings into yuan using local banks designated to \par \par \uc2  trade on the national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2      Trade is key to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply on the market \par \par \uc2  because the yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2      Dealers said there was no sign of the central People's Bank \par \par \uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Saturday. The bank had acted to support \par \par \uc2  the yuan on Thursday to prevent it from falling below 8.2800. \par \par \uc2      Analysts have said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had acted to stop it \par \par \uc2  falling out of the bottom end of the band partly to reduce \par \par \uc2  speculation on a possible devaluation. \par \par \uc2      But dealers said there were signs the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \par \par \uc2  reduced its activity over the past few months when the yuan \par \par \uc2  strayed out of the strong side of the band. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Dai Xianglong has said China is \par \par \uc2  considering a wider band and some economists suggest the move \par \par \uc2  could come before the end of the year. \par \par \uc2      But Dai has not given a timetable and many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts \par \par \uc2  forecast some widening of the range, currently less than a \par \par \uc2  fraction of one percent up or down, before the end of 2001. \par \par \uc2      On Saturday, the yuan weakened to 7.7300 against 100 \par \par \uc2  Japanese yen, compared with 7.6344 on Friday. It also ended \par \par \uc2  softer against the Hong Kong dollar to 1.0610 from 1.0607. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9u05zhu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
