\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends up in thin trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2000\par 06:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Sept 29 (Reuters) - China's yuan strengthened on Friday but trade slowed to a trickle ahead of a week-long holiday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2791 to one dollar, up from 8.2799 on Thursday, after moving in a range between 8.2790 and 8.2810. \par \par \uc2  The Shanghai-based national market will trade up to Saturday and then close until October 7 for a National Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said most bank traders stayed on the sidelines as transactions on Friday would be settled only after the holiday. \par \par \uc2  "Just a few transactions were done today," said a dealer at a foreign bank. "Bank traders only traded for urgent needs." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the thin trade made the exchange rate movements on Friday largely insignificant. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has traded near the weak end of its thin trading band this week as Chinese banks bought dollars for local importers, an indication of strong imports. \par \par \uc2  Chinese trading companies are required to convert most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings into yuan using local banks designated to trade on the national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Trade is key to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply on the market because the yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was expected to mainly move between 8.2790 and 8.2800 in thin trade on Saturday, the last trading day ahead of the holiday. \par \par \uc2  They said there was no evidence the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday. The bank acted to support the yuan on Thursday to prevent it from falling below the 8.2800 level - the weak end of its long-standing band. \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  from time to time over the past three years to keep the yuan in a thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  It has acted to stop it falling out of the bottom end of the band in part to quell speculation on a possible devaluation, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said there were signs the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had reduced its activity over the past few months as it stayed sidelined when the yuan strayed out of the strong side of the band. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Dai Xianglong has said China is considering a wider band and some economists suggest the move could come before the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  But Dai has not given a timetable and many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts forecast some widening of the range, currently less than a fraction of one percent up or down, before the end of 2001. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yuan strengthened to 7.6344 against 100 Japanese yen, compared with 7.6773 on Thursday. It also gained ground against the Hong Kong dollar to 1.0607 from 1.0616. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9t05v7q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
