\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan ends down, c.bank seen supporting. \b0\par\par\uc2 543 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2000\par 06:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Sept 28 (Reuters) - China's yuan lost ground on Thursday, coming under pressure as local banks bought dollars ahead of a long holiday, and dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in the session. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2799 to one dollar, down from 8.2795 on Wednesday after moving in a range between 8.2790 and 8.2801. \par \par \uc2  The Shanghai-based national market will trade up to Saturday and then close until October 7 for the National Day holidays. \par \par \uc2  "Domestic banks bought dollars to prepare for the long holiday," said a local bank dealer. "Their buying helped depress the yuan, though trade was thin." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were signs the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan in the last 30 minutes of trading, when it hovered below the 8.2800 level - the weak end of its long-standing band. \par \par \uc2  "There were signs that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was supporting the yuan today," a second local bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  The central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  from time to time over the past three years to force the yuan to close in a thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800, partly to quell speculation on a possible devaluation. It was last seen in the market on August 28. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said there were signs the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had reduced its activity over the past few months as it stayed sidelined when the yuan strayed out of the strong side the its normal trading band. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Dai Xianglong has said China is considering a wider band for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and some economists had expected the move could come before the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  For the time being, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed committed to preventing the yuan from falling below the weak end of its trading band to ease worries about a devaluation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong Financial Secretary Donald Tsang said this week that China was unlikely to introduce a larger or more flexible trading range for the yuan this year. \par \par \uc2  Most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts have forecast some widening of the range, currently less than a fraction of one percent up or down, before the end of 2001. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was likely to move between 8.2790 and 8.2800 in thin trade in the last two trading days ahead of the holiday and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to act again if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared ready to close below 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has traded near the weak end of its thin trading band this week as Chinese banks bought dollars for local importers, an indication China's imports are picking up. \par \par \uc2  Chinese trading companies are required to convert most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings into yuan using local banks designated to trade on the national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Trade is key to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply on the market because the yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the yuan strengthened to 7.6773 to 100 Japanese yen, compared with 7.7050 on Wednesday. It slipped slightly against the Hong Kong dollar to 1.0616 from 1.0614. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9s067cs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
