\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan weakens as importers snap up dollars. \b0\par\par\uc2 466 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2000\par 07:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Sept 25 (Reuters) - China's yuan weakened against the U.S. dollar on Monday due to increased dollar buying from domestic banks on behalf of local importers, a sign China's imports could be picking up, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2793 to one dollar, compared with 8.2785 on Friday after moving in a range between 8.2785 and 8.2796. \par \par \uc2  The yuan showed a brief bout of strength early last week, but that trend appeared to have ended as dollar demand from domestic importers gained steam, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Domestic banks have reported more dollar demand from importers and they bought dollars on the market," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Chinese trading companies are required to convert into yuan most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings through local banks designated to trade on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The yuan had moved near the strong end of its thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 for about two weeks before it began to fall back late last week. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said any strength in the yuan was likely to be temporary because China's imports had risen faster than exports over the past few months. \par \par \uc2  Trade is key to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply on the market because the yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  The yuan's weakness in the past few sessions coincided with interest rate reforms. Last Thursday, China freed rates on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lending and deposits of more than $3.0 million. \par \par \uc2  The government also raised the benchmark rate on one-year fixed U.S. dollar deposits of less than $3.0 million to 5.5 percent from five percent. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the rate moves had little effect on the yuan because China's rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlement system offered little room for speculation. \par \par \uc2  Over the past three years, the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan within the thin band of 8.2700 and 8.2800. It has said it was considering gradually widening the band but has given no timetable. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said there had been no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  since August 28, when it prevented the yuan from falling below the 8.2800 level. \par \par \uc2  They said the bank was likely to keep on the sidelines as there were few signs that the yuan would move outside its normal trading range this week. It was likely to hover around the 8.2790 level for the remainder of this week, they said. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the yuan strengthened at 7.6900 to 100 Japanese yen, compared with 7.7620 on Friday. It also gained ground against the Hong Kong dollar, climbing to 1.0610 from 1.0619. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9p06ns1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
