\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan creeps up, touches strong end of band. \b0\par\par\uc2 500 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2000\par 06:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Sept 15 (Reuters) - China's yuan strengthened against the U.S. dollar for the fifth straight day on Friday as dollar sales by local banks on behalf of exporters continued to underpin the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2771 compared with 8.2772 on Thursday after touching an intraday high of 8.2770 - the strong end of its normal thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800. It hit an intraday low of 8.2773. \par \par \uc2  There was no evidence the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan within the range, which it had enforced for three years until this spring, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has strengthened from 8.2790 at the end of last week, an indication that China's exports are picking up, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar selling from local banks on behalf of exporters continued to be the major factor today," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  Chinese exporters are required to sell most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings to local banks allowed to trade on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said China's exports appeared to have improved since the start of September after underperforming imports in August. \par \par \uc2  Foreign trade is key to dollar demand and supply on the market. China's yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan may close above the 8.2770 level next week, judging from its upward trend this week. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the yuan breached the record closing high of 8.2764 set on May 24, they said. \par \par \uc2  The bank was nowhere to be seen in May and June, when the yuan, buoyed by exports growth, closed outside the strong end of its band a dozen times. Analysts said this was a sign the bank was testing a broader trading range for the yuan. \par \par \uc2  The bank was last seen supporting the yuan on August 28 to prevent it closing below the key psychological 8.2800 level. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say the bank tends to take a harder line at the weak end of the long standing range to avoid speculation of a yuan devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the government would continue to pursue a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime in the longer term to prepare for shocks associated with entry to the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  China hopes to join the global trade body this year. \par \par \uc2  In a Reuters poll of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts in July, six out of 10 said the yuan could be trading in a range of plus or minus five percent or more by the end of 2001. \par \par \uc2  That would mark a major shift from the current de facto band of less than a fraction of one percent. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yuan closed stronger against the Japanese yen at 7.7124 to 100 yen, compared with 7.7181 on Thursday. It ended unchanged at 1.0609 against the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9f07sal\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
