\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends slightly lower after see-saw trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2000\par 06:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Sept 8 (Reuters) - China's yuan ended one notch down against the U.S. dollar in see-saw trade on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at its intraday high of 8.2790, compared with a 8.2789 finish on Thursday, and touched a session low of 8.2798. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar selling and buying orders came to the market sporadically, so the yuan ticked a M-shape line today," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar demand from domestic importers remained solid because the yuan continued wandering around the low end of its tiny trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800, which the central People's Bank of China has defended for nearly three years. \par \par \uc2  But there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, they said. \par \par \uc2  The bank was last seen supporting the yuan on August 28 to prevent it closing below the key psychological 8.2800 level due to \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent strong imports. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may re-emerge later this year as \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  believes its entry to the World Trade Organisation will put pressure on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounts. \par \par \uc2  "We both thought there might be some pressure and exports growth might be slower than imports" after Beijing joined the WTO, Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) chief executive Joseph Yam told reporters in Beijing. \par \par \uc2  Yam's remark was broadcast by Hong Kong Cable Television after he met Lu Nanping, a deputy director of the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the government may continue to pursue a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime after China joins the WTO, although such progress could be only gradual. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong said on Tuesday the government would consider widening the trading band of the yuan if necessary, but changes would not be too large, a Hong Kong television station reported. He gave no timetable. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yuan weakened against the yen to close at 7.9955, compared with 7.8005 on Thursday. It ended up at 1.0610 against the Hong Kong dollar from 1.0619. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9808qb0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
