\par\par \b \uc2  HKMA Gets No Move From China On Bank Asset Size Hurdle \b0\par\par\uc2 425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2000\par 07:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  could get no relaxation of China's US$20 billion asset requirement for banks wishing to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the mainland, said Chief Executive Joseph Yam. \par \par \uc2  After meetings with the People's Bank of China Governor Dai Xianglong in Beijing Tuesday Yam said efforts to have the hurdle lowered were unsuccessful. \par \par \uc2  A spokesperson with the HKMA said, however, that the PBOC is considering suggestions from a banking delegation accompanying Yam to allow the expansion of branch \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by those banks that had entered the mainland market before 1994, when the size rule was introduced, through the provision of a grandfather clause. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Bank of East Asia is prominent among local banks interested in some sort of relaxation of the rule. It has also been trying to boost its asset base in an effort to clear the size hurdle as it now stands, the bank's chairman, David Li, has said. \par \par \uc2  In comments before leaving for meetings with mainland officials Yam clearly doubted he could get any changes. Of the size limit he said "it is a convenient regulatory tool, and I do not see, at least in the foreseeable future, any likelihood of relaxation." \par \par \uc2  "I can understand...the constraints imposed by the terms of the (World Trade Organization) negotiations on any relaxation which may facilitate...entrance by smaller Hong Kong incorporated banks," said Yam. \par \par \uc2  In other matters, the HKMA chief executive also discussed the issue of a more flexible exchange rate regime for the Chinese yuan, an issue many believe the PBOC is taking under serious consideration. \par \par \uc2  Yam has been at pains, however, to make a clear distinction between more flexibility in the yuan exchange rate, and convertibility. Flexibility means the degree to which the exchange rate is allowed to fluctuate within a band before there is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Yam said. \par \par \uc2  However, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  now allows convertibility, or free exchange, only of the current account, that is, of trade flows. To be fully convertible, however, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be freely exchanged into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for trade, and for investment (capital account convertibility). "While most observers regard full convertibility as a desirable long-term goal, few believe that any significant moves will be made in that direction in the near term," said Yam recently. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA officials and the banking delegation will move on to Shanghai, from Beijing, Wednesday afternoon. \par \par \uc2  -By Eric Van Zant; (852) 2802-7002; \par \par \uc2  eric.vanzant@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9604dyh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
