\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan ends lower as exporter demand eases. \b0\par\par\uc2 365 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2000\par 06:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Sept 1 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed slightly lower against the U.S. dollar in thin trade on Friday as a lull in yuan selling from exporters helped the dollar rebound from losses in the previous two sessions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2788 to the dollar, down from 8.2784 on Thursday after hovering in a tight range of 8.2785 to 8.2788. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar rebounded as a sudden increased demand for the yuan from exporters in the previous two sessions eased," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  The yuan staged a brief recovery on Wednesday and Thursday after moving near the weaker end of its normal thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 over the past two months as China's imports picked up, creating increased demand for dollars. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were signs that China's imports continued to outpace exports and the yuan was likely to fall slightly further to the 8.2790 level in the near term. \par \par \uc2  They said there was no evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  came into the market on Thursday last week and on Monday to prevent the yuan from falling below 8.2800. For about three years, it has kept the yuan in a thin trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said that did not mean the government would change its aim of pursuing a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime for its looming membership of the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong said earlier this month the government was considering widening the yuan's trading range, but that it would take time to implement. \par \par \uc2  For the time being, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be keeping the yuan from falling below the 8.2800 level to prevent any speculation of a devaluation until a clearer picture of the WTO impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  emerged, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yuan closed lower at 7.9160 against 100 yen from 7.7440 on Thursday. It finished unchanged at 1.0610 against the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw91096m7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
