\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends up on exporter demand, trade thin. \b0\par\par\uc2 456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2000\par 06:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Aug 31 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed slightly higher against the U.S. dollar in thin trade on Thursday due to light demand from some exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2784 to the dollar, up from 8.2788 on Wednesday, after hovering in a tight range of 8.2783 to 8.2792. \par \par \uc2  "Some domestic banks bought the yuan on behalf of exporters, pushing up the exchange rate," one local bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "But buying was not heavy and trade was thin, indicating the rise today did not represent a trend," he said. \par \par \uc2  China's exporters are required to settle most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings with local banks designated to trade on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has been moving near the weaker end of its normal thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 over the past two months as China's imports picked up on the back of an improving economy, creating increased demand for dollars. \par \par \uc2  In July, China's imports surged a year-on-year 40.1 percent, outpacing a 24.1 percent rise in exports. Trade data are key for the yuan's exchange rates as it is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were signs that China's imports continued to outpace exports and the yuan was likely to fall slightly to around 8.2790 in the near term. \par \par \uc2  They said there was no evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  came into the market last Thursday and Monday to prevent the yuan from falling below 8.2800. For about three years, it has kept the yuan in a thin trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if the yuan breached 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  But they said that did not mean the government would change its aim of pursuing a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to upon its membership to the World Trade Organisation, which Beijing expects to occur this year. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong said earlier this month the government was considering widening the yuan's trading range, but that it would take time to implement. \par \par \uc2  For the time being, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be keeping the yuan from falling below the 8.2800 level to prevent any speculation of a devaluation until a clearer picture of the WTO impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  emerged, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the yuan closed higher at 7.7440 against 100 yen from 7.7797 on Wednesday. It finished marginally lower against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0610 from 1.0609. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw8v09h4j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
