\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan ends down on late dollar buying. \b0\par\par\uc2 428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2000\par 07:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Aug 25 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed a tad lower against the U.S. dollar on Friday, pushed by late buying of the American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by importers. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at an intraday low of 8.2800, compared with 8.2799 in the previous reason. It reached a intraday high of 8.2796 in early trade. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar supply and demand was largely balanced as the yuan wiggled between 8.2798 and 8.2799 during most of session before easing slightly at the market close. \par \par \uc2  There were no signs of the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was seen on Thursday, to keep the yuan from slipping through the key 8.2800 level, they said. \par \par \uc2  For almost three years, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has boxed the yuan in a razor-thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  "Compared with that of yesterday, dollar demand was much lighter today, more or less matching the supply," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan went down only at the final deal, so it did not represent the trend for the whole session." \par \par \uc2  China's rising imports have kept the yuan hovering around the 8.2800 level, the low end of its long-standing trading band, for nearly two months. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is convertible only on the current account and Chinese banks usually settle \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions after they trade hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with corporate clients. \par \par \uc2  China's latest trade data showed imports surged 40.1 percent year on year to $19.49 billion in July, outpacing a 24.1 percent rise in exports to $21.48 billion in the same period. \par \par \uc2  For the first time in a month, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in the market on Thursday to support the yuan, which was hammered down by a wave of dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action might re-emerge if strong imports, evidence of recovering economic growth, continued to put pressure on the yuan. \par \par \uc2  But the government aim of pursuing a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to prepare for entry to the World Trade Organisation was unlikely to change, they said. \par \par \uc2  Beijing may intend to protect the yuan to quench any speculation of a devaluation until there is a clearer picture of the impact of China's WTO entry on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they added. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the yuan closed down at 7.7364 against the yen from 7.7262. It finished up against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0610, compared with 1.0618 in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw8p0ahwe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
