\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan strengthens as exporters sell dollars. \b0\par\par\uc2 461 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2000\par 07:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Aug 8 (Reuters) - China's yuan gained some ground against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday, helped by late selling of the American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by exporters. \par \par \uc2  But the yuan, under pressure from strong dollar demand from domestic importers for more than a month, kept hovering around the weaker end of its long-standing trading range of 8.2770 and 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2798, up from 8.2800 in the previous session, after wriggling between 8.2790 and 8.2802. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were no signs the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push the yuan above the key 8.2800 support level, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long defended. \par \par \uc2  "It was not evident that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the market," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "It could be some banks coming in late to sell dollars" for their exporter clients, she said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the yuan's performance could indicate strong July imports as China's economy gained steam. Beijing is expected to release last month's trade figures soon. \par \par \uc2  Last week, a Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said the yuan could face downward pressure after China joined the World Trade Organisation, which is expected this year. \par \par \uc2  WTO membership could put pressure on the yuan as imports rise, said Zou Lin, vice director general of the balance of payment department of the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The administration manages China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  system. \par \par \uc2  Trade is a key factor driving the value of the yuan, which is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last seen supporting the yuan on July 24 and has not been active since. Still, dealers said they did not expect the bank to sit on its hands if stronger dollar demand weakened the yuan further. \par \par \uc2  They said, however, the cautious stance did not mean the government was backing away from pledges to relax its rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. \par \par \uc2  A a top \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official reaffirmed last week China's goal of making the yuan fully convertible after entry to the WTO. The official gave no specific timetable. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the government will make gradual changes and react cautiously to the yuan's weakening trend in order to quench speculation of a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the yuan ended up at 7.6550 against the Japanese yen from 7.7220. It closed unchanged at 1.0610 against the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2      ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  reutersh@uninet.com.cn) \par \par \uc2   SPEED GUIDES \par \par \uc2   RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  China news\tab \tab \tab    Shenzhen stock report\tab \par  Greater China news\tab \tab  Shanghai stock report\tab \par  Hong Kong news\tab \tab      Chinese hot stocks\tab \par  Taiwan news\tab \tab \tab   Chinese diary\tab \tab \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab      Chinese summary\tab \tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press digests .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dw8800f63\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
