\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan ends lower on importer dollar demand. \b0\par\par\uc2 394 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2000\par 05:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Aug 2 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed a touch lower against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday due to increased dollar demand from importers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2791 to the dollar, down from 8.2788 on Tuesday, after moving in a narrow range of 8.2789 and 8.2792. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan mainly traded at 8.2790 and 8.2791 on Wednesday, against 8.2788 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar demand from domestic importers remained strong," said a dealer at a local bank. \par \par \uc2  In the previous three weeks, the yuan settled near 8.2800 - the weaker end of its thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long enforced through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the weakness reflected increased dollar demand as \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's imports picked up in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  But they yuan had little room to fall further because there was ample dollar supply on the market as China's foreign trade surplus has surged this year, they said. \par \par \uc2  "At the present exchange rates, increased dollar demand from importers can easily be offset by a strong supply," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Trade is a key factor in the exchange rate of the yuan, which is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was likely to move narrowly around of 8.2790 in the near term. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had reduced its activity in the market in the past few months, an indication that Beijing might be moving towards more flexible yuan exchange rates. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong said last month the government was studying the possibility of widening the yuan's trading band, but gave no timetable. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the yuan closed lower at 7.7502 against the Japanese yen from 7.7012 on Tuesday. It also ended lower against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0620 from 1.0612. \par \par \uc2      ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  reutersh@uninet.com.cn) \par \par \uc2   SPEED GUIDES \par \par \uc2   RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  China news\tab \tab \tab    Shenzhen stock report\tab \par  Greater China news\tab \tab  Shanghai stock report\tab \par  Hong Kong news\tab \tab      Chinese hot stocks\tab \par  Taiwan news\tab \tab \tab   Chinese diary\tab \tab \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab      Chinese summary\tab \tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press digests .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dw8200vz8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
