\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan ends up on exporter demand. \b0\par\par\uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 August 2000\par 05:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Aug 1 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed a touch higher against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday on increased demand for the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2788 to one dollar, up from 8.2790 on Monday after moving in a narrow range of 8.2787 and 8.2791. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan mainly traded at 8.2788 on Tuesday, against 8.2792 and 8.2793 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "Local banks settled more \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions for exporters today, pushing the yuan up slightly," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Chinese trade firms are required to sell most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings to local banks designated to trade on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. They can buy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the banks when they need it for imports. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had reduced its activity on the market in the past few months, although it stepped in on Monday last week to prevent the yuan from straying outside the key 8.2800 psychological level. \par \par \uc2  They said the now rare \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not indicate Beijing would waver from its goal of a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong said last month the government was studying the possibility of widening the yuan's trading band but gave no timetable. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to be less flexible in allowing the yuan to push through the weaker side of the band in order to counter speculation of a yuan devaluation. \par \par \uc2  For the previous three weeks, the yuan has settled near 8.2800 - the weaker end of its thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long enforced through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan's weakness reflected increased dollar demand as China's imports picked up in recent weeks. But there was also ample dollar supply on the market as the foreign trade surplus has surged this year, they said. \par \par \uc2  Trade is a key factor in the exchange rate of the yuan, which is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was likely to move narrowly around of 8.2790 in the near term. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the yuan closed lower at 7.7012 against the Japanese yen from 7.5775 on Monday. It ended slightly higher against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0612 from 1.0616. \par \par \uc2      ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  reutersh@uninet.com.cn) \par \par \uc2   SPEED GUIDES \par \par \uc2   RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  China news\tab \tab \tab    Shenzhen stock report\tab \par  Greater China news\tab \tab  Shanghai stock report\tab \par  Hong Kong news\tab \tab      Chinese hot stocks\tab \par  Taiwan news\tab \tab \tab   Chinese diary\tab \tab \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab      Chinese summary\tab \tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press digests .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw8100ssb\p