\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends up as exporters sell dollars. \b0\par\par\uc2 335 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2000\par 06:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, July 27 (Reuters) - China's yuan gained ground against the U.S. dollar on Thursday as selling of the American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by local exporters picked up. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2791 to one dollar, up from 8.2795 on Wednesday. The yuan, convertible only on the current account, was sandwiched in a thin intraday range of 8.2787 and 8.2797. \par \par \uc2  "Unlike early this week, more banks cut dollar positions through the market today after their exporter customers sold the hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to them," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, for the first time since May, to keep the yuan from pushing through the lower end of its usual trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800, they said. \par \par \uc2  The long-defended 8.2800 mark was seen as a key psychological level, but the renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity early this week did not point to a change of Beijing's goal of moving gradually towards a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  range, analysts said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong said last week the government was studying the possibility of widening the yuan's trading band. He gave no timetable. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, was likely to be less flexible in allowing the yuan to slip through the weaker side of the band in order to quash fresh speculation of a yuan devaluation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the yuan closed up at 7.5200 against the Japanese yen from 7.5950 in the previous session. It ended one notch down to 1.0616 against the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2      ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  reutersh@uninet.com.cn) \par \par \uc2   SPEED GUIDES \par \par \uc2   RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  China news\tab \tab \tab    Shenzhen stock report\tab \par  Greater China news\tab \tab  Shanghai stock report\tab \par  Hong Kong news\tab \tab      Chinese hot stocks\tab \par  Taiwan news\tab \tab \tab   Chinese diary\tab \tab \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab      Chinese summary\tab \tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press digests .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw7r011ve\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
