\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan weakens a tad in slow trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2000\par 06:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, July 26 (Reuters) - China's yuan ended one notch lower against the U.S. dollar on Wednesday as dollar supply and demand evened out and trade slowed to a trickle. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2795 to one dollar, down from 8.2794 in the previous session. It was boxed in a slim range of 8.2787 and 8.2798. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar supply and demand roughly balanced in the market and trading was not active today," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday to hold the yuan above the key psychological level of 8.2800, which Beijing has long defended. \par \par \uc2  China's strong imports in June have driven down the yuan, which is convertible only on the current account. It has drifted near the 8.2800 mark for nearly three weeks. \par \par \uc2  Dealer said the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, for the first time since May, to keep the yuan from slipping through the lower end of the long-standing trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  But the renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity did not point to a change of Beijing's goal of moving gradually towards a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  range, they added. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is back into its usual thin range after closing outside the band's strong side of 8.2770 a dozen times in May and June. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the government could continue to test a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  range to prepare for entry to the World Trade Organisation, expected this year. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong said last week the government was studying the possibility of widening the yuan's trading band, but he gave no timetable. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to be less flexible in allowing the yuan to push through the weaker side of the band in order to quash fresh speculation of a yuan devaluation. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the yuan closed up at 7.5950 against the Japanese yen from 7.7252 a session ago. It also strengthened against the Hong Kong dollar to end at 1.0615, compared with 1.0619. \par \par \uc2      ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  reutersh@uninet.com.cn) \par \par \uc2   SPEED GUIDES \par \par \uc2   RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  China news\tab \tab \tab    Shenzhen stock report\tab \par  Greater China news\tab \tab  Shanghai stock report\tab \par  Hong Kong news\tab \tab      Chinese hot stocks\tab \par  Taiwan news\tab \tab \tab   Chinese diary\tab \tab \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab      Chinese summary\tab \tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press digests .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw7q017xu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
