\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends up,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  out of spotlight. \b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2000\par 07:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, July 25 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed higher against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday as strong importer demand for the American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased in late trade. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2794 to one dollar, up from 8.2798 in the previous session. It briefly touched an intraday low of 8.2800 in the midsession before dollar buying faded, helping lift the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, for the first time since May, to keep the yuan from slipping through the lower end of the long-standing trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long enforced that band through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Dollar buying turned inactive during late trading because most of that demand was met in early trade," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were few if any huge dollar selling orders piled around the 8.2800 level on Tuesday, the usual method the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to use in defending the yuan. \par \par \uc2  China's strong imports in June have driven down the yuan, convertible only on the current account. It has hovered near the key support level of 8.2800 for more than two weeks. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, dealers said there were signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan for the first time since May 19. \par \par \uc2  The renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity, however, did not point to a change of Beijing's stance to gradually move towards a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  range, they added. \par \par \uc2  Dealer said the the cautious \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have returned to the market to keep the yuan stable due to weakness of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since mid-July. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong said last week the government was studying the possibility of widening the yuan's trading band, but he gave no timetable. \par \par \uc2  Dai's remarks followed a one-month period up to June 23, during which the yuan had pushed through to close outside the 8.2770 level, the strong end of its band, a dozen times. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Beijing could continue to test a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  range to prepare for entry to the World Trade Organisation, expected this year. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to be more flexible in allowing the yuan to push through the strong side of the band, because Beijing may want to quash fresh speculation of a yuan depreciation. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the yuan closed down at 7.7252 against the Japanese yen from 7.6014 a session ago. It ended up at 1.0619 against the Hong Kong dollar, compared with 1.0625. \par \par \uc2      ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  reutersh@uninet.com.cn) \par \par \uc2   SPEED GUIDES \par \par \uc2   RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  China news\tab \tab \tab    Shenzhen stock report\tab \par  Greater China news\tab \tab  Shanghai stock report\tab \par  Hong Kong news\tab \tab      Chinese hot stocks\tab \par  Taiwan news\tab \tab \tab   Chinese diary\tab \t