\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends unchanged in sluggish trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2000\par 06:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, July 12 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed unchanged at 8.2795 against the U.S. dollar in thin trade on Wednesday, the third session this week it stuck to that level. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said trading interest eased, boxing the yuan into a tiny range of 8.2793 and 8.2797, compared with an already slim band of 8.2785 and 8.2797 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The market was quiet today and only a small number of deals were done," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the People's Daily quoted Foreign Trade Ministry figures as saying that China's contracted foreign direct investment rose a year-on-year 24.6 percent to $24.17 billion in the first half of this year. \par \par \uc2  The report did not immediately affect China's tightly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where banks trade \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  mainly for settlement of imports and exports. \par \par \uc2  The newspaper said actual FDI in the period fell 7.5 percent year-on-year to $17.167 billion. The report gave no details. \par \par \uc2  Although actual FDI declined, dealers said net foreign capital inflows had been factored into the yuan's earlier strength. \par \par \uc2  The yuan had risen steadily for more than one month since early May, largely due to China's healthy trade surplus. It is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  FDI is an item on the capital account and not a major driver affecting the yuan, but had helped lift the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as foreign investors must buy yuan to fund local \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The contracted FDI figure, a reflection of the trend of future foreign capital inflow, may continue to help underpin the yuan, they said. \par \par \uc2  The strongest influence on the yuan comes from China's trade performance. Since late June, the yuan has weakened for more than two weeks, which could be a sign of strong imports during recent months, dealers have said. \par \par \uc2  China is expected to release its foreign trade data for June and the first half of 2000 by early next week. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has been back in its usual thin trading band of 8.2770 and 8.2800 since June 23 after a month during which it closed outside that range a dozen times. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  used to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan in that tight range. \par \par \uc2  The Economic Daily newspaper said the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  was relaxing its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, effectively allowing the yuan to fluctuate more under market forces. \par \par \uc2  The story appeared to be an indirect acknowledgement - the first in the official media - of analyst views that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is testing a wider trading range for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the country's entry to the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Wednesday. The bank was last seen in the market on May 19. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed up at 7.7188 against the Japanese yen from 7.7285 on Tuesday. It also finished ended one notch higher at 1.0617 against the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2      ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  reutersh@uninet.com.cn) \par \par \uc2   SPEED GUIDES \par \par \uc2   RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  China news\tab \tab \tab    Shenzhen stock report