\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan ends down on interest payment demand. \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2000\par 06:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, July 3 (Reuters) - China's yuan weakened further against the U.S. dollar on Monday as demand for dollars from companies paying half yearly interest on foreign loans remained strong. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2787 to the dollar, the lowest closing level since May 9 and down from 8.2782 on Friday. It moved in a narrow range of 8.2782 to 8.2788 during Monday's session. \par \par \uc2  "Chinese companies typically pay interest on their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  debts every six months," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  "This has led to a temporary increase of dollar demand on the market, making the yuan go down steadily in the past few sessions," he said. \par \par \uc2  Despite the latest fall, dealers said there was a strong supply of dollars on the market because of China's healthy trade surplus and the yuan was likely to rebound soon. \par \par \uc2  China had a trade surplus of $10.46 billion in the first five months of this year, up from $7.07 billion a year earlier. The surplus is a key factor affecting dollar supply on the market as the yuan is convertible only on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Before weakening last week, the yuan had strengthened for nearly two months, ending outside its normal wafer-thin band of 8.2780 and 8.2800 a dozen times. The central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  used to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan in that tight range. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last seen in the market on May 19. \par \par \uc2  Analysts and dealers say the government is experimenting cautiously with a wider trading band for the yuan. \par \par \uc2  China needs more exchange rate flexibility to prepare for its entry into the World Trade Organisation, which Beijing expects to happen this year, they say. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was expected to rebound soon and strengthen outside its old narrow range again. The near-term resistance for a closing level was seen at 8.2760, they said. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the yuan closed stronger at 7.8001 against the yen from 7.8439 on Friday. It inched up to 1.0612 against the Hong Kong dollar from 1.0613. \par \par \uc2      ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  reutersh@uninet.com.cn) \par \par \uc2   SPEED GUIDES \par \par \uc2   RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  China news\tab \tab \tab    Shenzhen stock report\tab \par  Greater China news\tab \tab  Shanghai stock report\tab \par  Hong Kong news\tab \tab      Chinese hot stocks\tab \par  Taiwan news\tab \tab \tab   Chinese diary\tab \tab \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab      Chinese summary\tab \tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press digests .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw7302jxh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
