\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends at three-week low in active trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2000\par 07:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, June 29 (Reuters) - China's yuan fell to a three-week low against the U.S. dollar on Thursday, closing at 8.2780 on increasing dollar demand from importers. \par \par \uc2  The yuan moved in a weaker range of 8.2776 and 8.2790 and dealers said more banks were buying dollars due to heavy demand from importers. The yuan closed at 8.2776 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar demand is rising steadily these days because many importers have been buying dollars at their banks," said a dealer at a local bank. \par \par \uc2  The yuan, which has strengthened for nearly two months as China posted healthy trade surpluses from January to May, is convertible only on the current account, comprised mainly of trade flows. \par \par \uc2  But imports in the first five months of this year also surged 35.4 percent year-on-year to $81.82 billion and analysts say they expect the momentum to continue. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended its rising streak and fell back to it usual 8.2770 and 8.2800 range on Monday after closing for the 12th time since May 23 outside the narrow band last Friday. \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of China had kept the yuan within the hair-thin band through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than two years. \par \par \uc2  But analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been testing the waters for more flexibility in the exchange rate ahead of its entry to the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  The yuan's retreat back into its usual range coincided with recent media reports on Beiing's yuan policy. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, China's Vice Minister of Finance Jin Liqun said he was comfortable with the "managed float" \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund denied a Hong Kong newspaper report that it asked Beijing to float the yuan as early as the last quarter of this year. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, Hong Kong's de facto central banker Joseph Yam quoted China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Dai Xianglong as saying Beijing had no plans to allow the yuan to float within a wider trading band in the short term. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said there had been no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to rein in the yuan. They said the yuan could again sway out of the range in the near term. \par \par \uc2  "There is still room for the yuan to move wider because the limits set by the trading centre has not been reached," said a dealer at a foreign bank in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  According to the trading rules at the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading system, the yuan's daily movement against the U.S. dollar is capped at 0.3 percent above or below the weighted average price of the previous trading day. \par \par \uc2  That limit against the Hong Kong dollar is one percent and for the Japanese yen it is 10 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the limits had barely been tested due to the tight control the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has maintained. \par \par \uc2  "If the yuan trades within 8.2770 and 8.2800 throughout the second half of this year, then we can say Bejing may not attempt to widen the band," the foreign bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the yuan closed lower at 7.8880 against the yen from 7.8656. It ended up at 1.0616 against the Hong Kong dollar from 1.0713. \par \par \uc2      ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  reutersh@uninet.com.cn) \par \par \uc2   SPEED GUIDES \par \par \uc2   RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  China news\tab \tab \