\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-China aims for full yuan convertibility. \b0\par\par\uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2000\par 09:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, June 15 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Dai Xianglong said on Thursday that China would push ahead gradually with making its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fully convertible after entry to the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  "With China's accession to the WTO, we will press ahead with the capital account convertibility process gradually," Dai told a forum on economic globalisation in Beijing. \par \par \uc2  China hopes to join the world trade club this year. The yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now convertible only on the current account, comprised mainly of trade-related flows. \par \par \uc2  China would maintain the value of the yuan as the economy continued to favour a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Dai, governor of the People's Bank of China. \par \par \uc2  "China's economy continues to grow fast with a favourable balance of payments and improved financial supervision," he said. "This will continue to ensure a stable renminbi (yuan)." \par \par \uc2  Analysts say China has already introduced more flexibility in its exchange rate by allowing the yuan to drift outside its usual trading band of 8.2770 to 8.2800 against the U.S. dollar on the national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the yuan closed unchanged at 8.2768 to the dollar, the ninth time it has finished a day's trade outside the usual slim band since May 23. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Beijing maintains that the market sets the exchange rate of the yuan, although in practice the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually keeps the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  locked in a narrow range through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  STABLE YUAN HELPS GROWTH \par \par \uc2  Dai said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  would improve its "single, managed, floating exchange rate regime." He gave no details. \par \par \uc2  Dai said a sound monetary policy and a stable yuan would help promote ecomomic growth. \par \par \uc2  He estimated China's gross domestic product rose around eight percent in the first half of this year. \par \par \uc2  China's GDP rose 8.1 percent year-on-year in the first quarter and the government has officially forecast GDP growth of around seven percent this year against 7.1 percent last year. \par \par \uc2  "China has already shaken off the negative impact of the Asian financial crisis," Dai said. \par \par \uc2  As China opens further to the outside world, the People's Bank would also move ahead with interest rate liberalisation, allowing deposit and lending rates to be set by the market, he said, but gave no timetable. \par \par \uc2  Chinese banks can now only levy interest rates on loans of 10 percent above or below a base level set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  ((Beijing Newsroom +8610 6586-5566 ext 202, Fax +8610 8527-5258 beijing.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6f03k64\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
