\par\par \b \uc2  China  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market turnover up in May. \b0\par\par\uc2 348 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2000\par 06:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, June 8 (Reuters) - Turnover on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market surged more than 80 percent year on year to the equivalent of $3.168 billion in May, the official Shanghai Securities News reported on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Average daily trading turnover on the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System hit $176 million equivalent in May, representing a surge of 82.7 percent from May 1999, the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  The breakdown for May this year was $3.094 billion, HK$275 million and 4.27 billion yen, it said. The market only trades the three foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  China's yuan was up at 8.2773 to one U.S. dollar at the end of May against 8.2799 at the end of April, the newspaper said without giving further details. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the turnover surge reflected China's improving foreign trade this year, which led to more \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlements from local banks on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  In the first four months of this year, China's exports surged 39 percent and imports rose more than 38 percent year on year, according to official figures. China reported a foreign trade surplus of $7.3 billion in the January-April period. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan rose in May due to China's surging exports and healthy foreign trade surplus. \par \par \uc2  The yuan set a record closing high of 8.2764 on May 24, ending at its strongest level since China unified its dual exchange rates and set up the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 1994. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has closed outside its usually rigid range of 8.2770 to 8.2780 five times in May without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts see the unusual movements as an indication the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is testing a broader range for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prepare for China's entry into the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  Officials at the People's Bank of China in Beijing declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 reutersh@uninet.com.cn). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6803zdd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
