\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan ends lower on importer dollar demand. \b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2000\par 06:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, June 5 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed slightly lower in thin trade against the U.S. dollar on Monday as domestic banks conducted light dollar buying on behalf of local importers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  It finished at 8.2776 to one dollar against 8.2773 on Friday after hitting an intraday high of 8.2757 and a low of 8.2777. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were no signs that the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has closed outside its usual rigid range of 8.2770 to 8.2780 five times in the past two weeks. \par \par \uc2  "Some domestic banks have seen more dollar demand from importers recently and they settled it on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued keeping on the sidelines, leaving market forces to decide the yuan's trend," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the last time the bank was seen in the market was May 19, two trading days before the yuan started to stray out of its thin range. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has for years \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within that band. \par \par \uc2  Analysts see the unusual movements in the yuan as an indication the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is testing a broader range for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prepare for China's entry into the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say they are getting used to the movements. \par \par \uc2  "We believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has somewhat changed its stand," said another local bank dealer. "We will not be surprised next time when the yuan drifts slightly out of the controlled trading band." \par \par \uc2  Officials at the People's Bank of China in Beijing declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  set a record closing high of 8.2764 on May 24, ending at its strongest level since China unified its dual exchange rates in 1994. It had strengthened for a month, supported by China's healthy foreign trade surplus, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  China posted a surplus of $7.3 billion for the first four months of this year, up from $5.22 billion for the first quarter, official figures showed. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said exports were expected to grow steadily through the summer, which should continue to underpin the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed lower against the Japanese yen at 7.6730 to 100 yen from 7.6100 on Friday. It ended a touch higher at 1.0614 against the Hong Kong dollar compared with 1.0618. \par \par \uc2     ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  reutersh@uninet.com.cn)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw65049be\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
