\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-China yuan ends up, steps outside band. \b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2000\par 06:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, June 1 (Reuters) - China's yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed higher against the U.S. dollar on Thursday on strong buying by exporters, ending outside of its narrow trading band for the fifth time in two weeks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  China requires trading firms to sell most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings back to state banks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which typically keeps the local unit in a range of 8.2770 to 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2769 to the U.S. dollar, up from 8.2771 on Wednesday and a whisker outside the usual trading range. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to be testing a wider trading band ahead of China's entry to the World Trade Organisation, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank has declined to comment, although an official of the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System, which runs the market, confirmed Beijing was exploring a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  "The recent yuan moves look more market-driven, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been active in the market," said a dealer at a local bank. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last seen in the market on May 19, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan set a record closing high of 8.2764 last Wednesday, ending at its strongest level since China unified its dual exchange rates in 1994. That close broke the record set on the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in the short term, allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  greater room to move under market forces before entry to the world trade club. \par \par \uc2  The yuan was expected to strengthen, supported by China's healthy foreign trade surplus, they said. \par \par \uc2  China posted a trade surplus of $7.3 billion for the first four months of this year, up from $5.22 billion for the same period last year, official figures showed. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said exports were expected to grow steadily through the summer, which should support the yuan. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended up against the Japanese yen at 7.6706, compared with 7.7747 on Wednesday. It inched down marginally against the Hong Kong dollar, ending at 1.0615. \par \par \uc2  ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015; reutersh@uninet.com.cn). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6104c5a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
