\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-China yuan closes outside band, third time in week. \b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2000\par 06:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, May 26 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed higher against the dollar on Friday on strong demand from exporters, the third time this week that the tightly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended outside its usual thin trading band. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be testing a wider trading band for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the country's entry to the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at an intraday high of 8.2769 to the U.S. dollar, up from 8.2770 on Thursday and outside the narrow range of 8.2770 to 8.2800 in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually keeps it. \par \par \uc2  It touched a low of 8.2774. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to have quietly acknowledged a slightly wider trading range for the yuan," said a local bank dealer. "We believe the new band extends the upside limit to 8.2760." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials declined to comment on Thursday. Bank officials told Reuters in February they were considering a widening of the yuan's trading band to prepare for WTO entry but gave no details or timetable. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  set a closing record of 8.2764 on Wednesday, finishing at its highest level since China unified its dual exchange rates in 1994. That close broke the record set on Tuesday. There was no evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on either day, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Another dealer at a local bank said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be taking advantage of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent strength to experiment with the wider band, since testing it on the weak end could be destabilising. The yuan closed at a 28-month low on April 12, ending outside the band at 8.2830. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan had little downside pressure at present as China's exports had been strong in the past few months. \par \par \uc2  China's exports rose a year-on-year 39 percent to $72.2 billion in the first four months of this year and the country recorded a strong trade surplus of $7.3 billion in the period. \par \par \uc2  A Beijing based dealer at a local bank said he expected the yuan's strength to allow for a gradual widening of the band to 8.2760, and perhaps on to 8.2750 if upward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued. \par \par \uc2  "It will be a slow, gradual process, not a sudden move," she said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign trade data are key to the yuan's exchange rates because they decide dollar supply and demand on the market. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only convertible on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan also won a boost on Thursday from figures on foreign investment in China. \par \par \uc2  China's contracted foreign direct investment rose a year-on-year 28.2 percent to $14.643 billion in the first four months of this year, the China Securities newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said China's WTO membership could have an impact on the yuan in the long term. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to come under pressure as China's demand for dollars was due to pick up on rising imports after it joined the global trade group, they said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. House of Representatives on Wednesday approved a bill that would grant permanent normal trade relations (PNTR) to China. Passage in the Senate is virtually assured by mid-June. \par \par \uc2  The House vote paves the way for China's smooth entry to the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  The yuan finished slightly lower against the Japanese yen at 7.6653 to 100 yen compared with 7.6335 on Thursday. It inched higher to 1.0612 against the Hong Kong dollar from 1.0615. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw5q04vap\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
