\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan opens down, unmoved by PNTR. \b0\par\par\uc2 437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2000\par 04:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, May 25 (Reuters) - The Chinese yuan opened slightly lower on Thursday, pressured by light technical selling after record high closes over the past two days and unmoved by the U.S. House approval of a key China trade bill, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. House on Wednesday approved a bill that would grant permanent normal trade relations (PNTR) to China. Passage in the Senate is virtually assured by mid-June. \par \par \uc2  The House vote paves the way for China's smooth entry to the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  The yuan was trading weaker at 8.2772 to the U.S. dollar by 0224 GMT. It closed on Wednesday at a record high of 8.2764 to the U.S. dollar, outside the razor-thin band in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  normally keeps it. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the short-term effect on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was limited as the market had already factored in the PNTR news. \par \par \uc2  "As China's yuan is not convertible on the capital account and is under tight control of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , China's WTO membership can only have an impact on the yuan in the long term," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "The market had expected the news," he added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was pressured by technical selling in early trading on Thursday after its gains in the past two days. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at record highs on Tuesday and Wednesday, fuelling speculation the central People's Bank of China was testing a wider trading band for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of China's WTO entry. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  on either day. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  normally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan in a tight trading band between 8.2770 and 8.2800. \par \par \uc2  China's exports showed a strong upward trend in the past few months, strengthening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and dealers said there would be little downside pressure in the near term, they said. \par \par \uc2  China's exports rose a year-on-year 39 percent to $72.2 billion in the first four months of this year and the country recorded a strong trade surplus of $7.3 billion in the period. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said China's yuan was likely to come under pressure in the longer term as China's demand for dollars due to imports picks up after it joins the WTO. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected the yuan to close slightly lower on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 email:reutersh@uninet.com.cn)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw5p04yse\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
