\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 3-China shares up on PNTR but yuan unfazed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Brian Rhoads \par \uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2000\par 12:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, May 25 (Reuters) - China stocks advanced on Thursday, lifted by a U.S. House vote to grant permanent normal trade relations (PNTR) to China, and extending a rally that began last week. \par \par \uc2  Investors sought counters of port and shipping companies on expectations their business would see a boom once China joins the World Trade Organisation. But gains were capped since much of the PNTR news had already been factored in. \par \par \uc2  The insulated yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was largely unaffected by the PNTR vote, ending lower against the dollar after two days of record high closes. \par \par \uc2  The Shanghai B share index rose 0.57 percent to 46.964, back from its midday losses but off a session high of 47.919. Turnover eased to $14 million. \par \par \uc2  The Shenzhen B share index finished one percent higher at 94.16 on lower turnover of HK$52.62 million. \par \par \uc2  Domestic A share indices in both cities also rose on the PNTR vote, closing up more than one percent at record highs. \par \par \uc2  HOUSE VOTE \par \par \uc2  The House of Representatives voted 237 to 197 overnight to grant PNTR to China, a step that would guarantee Chinese goods the same low-tariff access to U.S. markets as other nations. \par \par \uc2  The bill now goes to the Senate where passage is virtually assured, paving the way for China's smooth entry to the WTO. China expects to join the global trade group this year. \par \par \uc2  "The PNTR passage added fresh power to beef up market sentiment," said a broker at Guotai J&A Securities. \par \par \uc2  But the mild market gains on Thursday surprised few brokers. Much of the news relating to the WTO had been factored in after Beijing's trade deal with the European Union last week. \par \par \uc2  Shares have risen since mid-May on optimism the EU and China would reach a deal on the country's WTO entry. That deal was clinched last Friday. \par \par \uc2  By Thursday's close, Shanghai's B share market had risen more than 14 percent from May 15 - the Monday ahead of the EU deal - while Shenzhen's B shares were up more than 11 percent. \par \par \uc2  Shanghai and Shenzhen A shares have gained more than nine percent over the same period. \par \par \uc2  SHIPPING IN SPOTLIGHT \par \par \uc2  Port and shipping counters came under the market spotlight on Thursday. B shares of Shanghai-listed Tianjin Marine Shipping Co rose four percent to $0.208 in active trade. \par \par \uc2  The company's domestic A share was among top gainers, surging 9.97 percent to 10.81 yuan on heavy volume of 9.50 million shares. \par \par \uc2  In Shenzhen, Chiwan Wharf Co was one of the most heavily traded B shares after 1.12 million shares changed hands. It closed up HK$0.11 or 5.09 percent at HK$2.27. \par \par \uc2  The Shanghai A share index added 1.37 percent to 2,000.079 while the Shenzhen A share index put on 1.32 percent to 624.80. Both closes were record highs for the indices. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said China's markets were expected to gain further ground in the near term, though any rise in tensions between China and Taiwan could hang over the market. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the yuan closed back inside its regular trading range at 8.2770 to one U.S. dollar on thin volume. On Wednesday it closed at a record high of 8.2764, outside the razor-thin band in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  normally allows it to move. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the short-term effect on the yuan was limited. \par \par \uc2  "As China's yuan is not convertible on the capital account and is under tight control of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , China's WTO membership can only have an impact on the yuan in the long term," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Record high closes on Tuesday and Wednesday have fuelled speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was testing a wider trading band for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's WTO entry. \par \par \uc2  The bank normally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan in a tight trading band between 8.2770 and 8.2800. \par \par \uc2   Hong Kong report \par \par \uc2   Taiwan report \par \par \uc2   Shanghai indices \par \par \uc2   Shenzhen indices \par \par \uc2   Hong Kong indices \par \par \uc2   H-share index \par \par \uc2   Red share index \par \par \uc2   SPEED GUIDES \par \par \uc2   RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  China news\tab \tab \tab  All equity news\tab \tab \par  Greater China news\tab      Chinese summary\tab \tab \par  Hong Kong news\tab \tab    Chinese hot stocks\tab \par  Taiwan news\tab \tab \tab China IPOs\tab \tab \par  Chinese diary\tab \tab     Hong Kong IPOs\tab \tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press digests .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw5p04t5l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
