\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan eases in sleepy trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 381 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2000\par 06:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, April 14 (Reuters) - China's yuan ended two notches lower against the greenback on Friday as purchases of U.S. dollars by importers rose slightly. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2793 to one dollar against 8.2791 on Thursday, one day after finishing at a 28-month closing low of 8.2830. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan started to replay its dull trading, so fewer people are speculating that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may kick start its band-widening plan soon." said a dealer at a local bank. \par \par \uc2  Much to market's surprise, the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan on Wednesday. That fuelled speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's consideration of a wider band for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might become more than just a proposal. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters in February the government was considering a gradual widening of the trading band as China pursued its bid to join the World Trade Organisation, but he gave no timetable. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the yuan swiftly bounced back above the key 8.2800 level and went back to moving its usual tight, government-imposed daily range. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday and \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign trade performance would continue to drive the price of the yuan, which is not convertible on the capital account. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar buying by importers was slightly up today," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  China's exports rose 39.1 percent year on year to $51.72 billion in the first quarter of 2000, coming off a low base in the same period of 1999. Imports rose 41 percent to $46.5 billion. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan was expected to move between 8.2770 and 8.2800 next week. \par \par \uc2  The yuan firmed against the Japanese yen to 7.6850 to 100 yen from 7.8199 on Thursday. It edged down against the Hong Kong dollar to 1.0632 from 1.0627. \par \par \uc2  ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  reutersh@uninet.com.cn) \par \par \uc2  SPEED GUIDES \par \par \uc2  RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China news\tab \tab \tab      Shenzhen stock report\tab \par Greater China news\tab \tab    Shanghai stock report\tab \par Hong Kong news\tab \tab \tab  Chinese hot stocks\tab \par Taiwan news\tab \tab \tab     Chinese diary\tab \tab \par All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab \tab  Chinese summary\tab \tab \par Press digests\tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab     .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4e02e4x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
