\par\par \b \uc2  Chinese yuan recovers from 28-month low. \b0\par\par\uc2 487 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2000\par 07:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, April 13 (Reuters) - One day after hitting a 28-month low, China's yuan\tab \tab pushed back to end higher on Thursday, the market returning to normal trade within its tight, government-imposed band.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yuan closed at 8.2791 to the U.S. dollar, sharply up from 8.2830 on Wednesday, sandwiched in a five-notch range of 8.2791 to 8.2796.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  But dealers were still talking about Wednesday's slide, which they said may have been a test of market reaction to a proposed widening of the trading band by Beijing. \par \par \uc2  Much to market's surprise, the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan on Wednesday, allowing it to close below the key 8.2800 level. \par \par \uc2  That fuelled speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's consideration of a wider band for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might become more than just a proposal. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters in February the government was considering a gradual widening of the trading band as China pursued its bid to join the World Trade Organisation, but he gave no timetable. \par \par \uc2  "Everything seemed to cool down today, and the bid/ask spread contracted to a very thin one," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  "Supply and demand were largely balanced in the morning." \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said Thursday's razor-thin band may have indicated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was back in the market. \par \par \uc2  The issue of whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be devaluated is still in the market spotlight, despite Asia's regional recovery from the economic crisis that hit in 1997. But dealers said a wider band would not necessarily lead to a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  The government has said consistently, and repeated its view on Wednesday, that there is no reason to devalue the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said the yuan would be strongly supported in the near term by China's strong trade figures. \par \par \uc2  China's exports rose 39.1 percent year on year to $51.72 billion in the first quarter of 2000, coming off a low base in the same period of 1999, Xinhua news agency said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Imports also shot up, rising 41 percent to $46.5 billion. \par \par \uc2  China's trade surplus for the first quarter was 5.22 billion, compared to $4.275 billion in the same period of 1999. \par \par \uc2  But Zhou Keren, vice foreign trade minister, urged caution against being too optimistic over China's rapid exports growth. \par \par \uc2  "The rapid export growth was largely a recovery from a low comparative base," official media quoted Zhou as telling a trade meeting. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they would closely monitor the yuan movement in the near term, watching for signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  intentions. \par \par \uc2  The yuan rose against the Japanese yen to 7.8199 to 100 yen from 7.8864 on Wednesday. It ended flat against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0627. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4d02iv6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
