\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - China ponders loosening yuan trading bands. \b0\par\par\uc2 699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2000\par 09:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, Feb 25 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering wider trading bands for the yuan, a bank spokesman said on Friday, a move which economists said could lead to an appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The spokesman said there was no timetable for the change, which has been widely anticipated as part of financial market liberalisation linked to China's bid to join the World Trade Organisation this year. \par \par \uc2  "We are considering widening the yuan fluctuation band gradually," the spokesman for the People's Bank of China told Reuters. "But I can't give you any timetable." \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Hong Kong's leftwing Ta Kung Pao newspaper quoted an unidentified "authoritative person" as saying Beijing would seek to widen the trading band this year. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is now virtually pegged to the U.S. dollar and on Friday closed at 8.2785 compared with 8.2780 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Economists said that even with looser trading bands the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would still retain tight control over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the capital account is closed. Speculators cannot get their hands on enough yuan to manipulate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  During the Asian economic crisis, which began in 1997 when Thailand floated the baht, all eyes were on China because of fears that a yuan devaluation would deepen the turmoil. \par \par \uc2  But China is now running a healthy trade surplus, putting upward pressure on the yuan. \par \par \uc2  STRONG GROWTH SUPPORTS YUAN \par \par \uc2  "We've been thinking for a while that they are going to widen the bands and we actually feel it will lead to an appreciation," Steve Brice, treasury economist at Standard Chartered in Singapore, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "Although we are looking at a wider band, we are not looking at a change to the capital controls, so the yuan will still go pretty much where the authorities want it to," he said. \par \par \uc2  A senior dealer at a Japanese bank in Singapore also predicted a stronger yuan. \par \par \uc2  "I think this is actually going to amount to a revaluation rather than a devaluation," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "Real growth prospects in China are good," the dealer added. "Relative to the rest of Asia the yuan should carry on its strengthening trend." \par \par \uc2  China's exports surged 47.8 percent year-on-year to $16.8 billion in January, producing a trade surplus of $1.5 billion. \par \par \uc2  Last year, China's trade surplus was $30 billion, down from $43.6 billion in 1998. \par \par \uc2  To maintain the yuan's stability, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  now \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tightly-controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling or buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Since WTO is coming, they will have to take steps to make banks and the whole system more flexible and a convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is part of it," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a European bank in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  "They will do it very slowly without devaluing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , just letting it move up and down a little more. This year is possible," he said. \par \par \uc2  CHANGE CONSIDERED FOR YEARS \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters the government had been contemplating a wider trading band for the yuan for several years, though preparatory work halted briefly in 1998 due to the Asian economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  Chinese bankers said there were no signs a more flexible yuan policy would be implemented immediately. \par \par \uc2  The European bank dealer said the change "is very likely" and was a political necessity as well as an unavoidable step towards making the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fully convertible. \par \par \uc2  "If you want to move to a convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , people first have to get used to some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. This is a way to let people know that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not fixed forever," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "It's also political. China doesn't want its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too much pegged to the U.S. dollar. It's a better story to have your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuating with whatever happens in the market." \par \par \uc2  The yuan is convertible on the current account and Beijing has said it would open the capital account eventually. It has not given a timetable. \par \par \uc2  ((Charlie Zhu, Beijing Newsroom +8610 6586-5566 ext 202, Fax +8610 8527-5258 beijing.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw2p06fsr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
