\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - China mulling wider yuan trading band. \b0\par\par\uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2000\par 06:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, Feb 25 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it is considering a move to allow the yuan to trade within a wider band as Beijing bids to join the World Trade Organisation (WTO). \par \par \uc2  "We are considering widening the yuan fluctuation band gradually," said a spokesman for the People's Bank of China spokesman. "But I can't give you any timetable." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said China was expected to widen the band this year. \par \par \uc2  "Since WTO is coming, they will have to take steps to make banks and the whole system more flexible and a convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is part of it," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a European bank in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  "They will do it very slowly without devaluing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , just letting it move up and down a little more. This year is possible," he said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is virtually pegged to the U.S. dollar. Chinese bankers said some companies, especially exporters, were eager for a wider trading band as they expected the yuan to fall, which would make their products cheaper overseas. \par \par \uc2  To keep the yuan stable, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  now \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tightly-controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling or buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed lower against the dollar on Friday as domestic banks sold the yuan on increased dollar demand from importers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  It ended at 8.2785 to one U.S. dollar from 8.2780 on Thursday after moving in a range of 8.2780 and 8.2787. \par \par \uc2  CHANGE CONSIDERED FOR YEARS \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters the government had been contemplating a wider trading band for the yuan for several years, though preparatory work halted briefly in 1998 due to the Asian economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Hong Kong's leftwing Ta Kung Pao newspaper quoted an unidentified "authoritative person" as saying Beijing would seek to widen the trading band this year. \par \par \uc2  The paper said Beijing would not execute a "policy-induced" yuan devaluation. \par \par \uc2  During the Asian crisis, which began in 1997 when Thailand floated the baht, all eyes were on China because of fears that a devaluation of the yuan would deepen the crisis. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say, however, that with the Asian economies now recovering, a yuan devaluation now would not have that much impact and Beijing needed a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost its exports and the economy. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts say the yuan faces appreciation this year due to China's healthy trade surplus. \par \par \uc2  China's exports surged 47.8 percent year-on-year to $16.8 billion in January, producing a trade surplus of $1.5 billion. \par \par \uc2  Last year, China's trade surplus was $30 billion, down from $43.6 billion in 1998. \par \par \uc2  POLITICAL NEED AS WELL \par \par \uc2  Chinese bankers said there were no signs a more flexible yuan policy would be implemented immediately. \par \par \uc2  The European dealer said the change "is very likely" and was a political necessity as well as an unavoidable step towards making the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fully convertible. \par \par \uc2  "If you want to move to a convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , people first have to get used to some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. This is a way to let people know that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not fixed forever," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "It's also political. China doesn't want its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too much pegged to the U.S. dollar. It's a better story to have your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuating with whatever happens in the market." \par \par \uc2  The yuan is convertible on the current account and Beijing has said it would open the capital account eventually. It has not given a timetable. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed lower against the Japanese yen at 7.4800 to 100 yen on Friday compared with 7.3898 on Thursday. It ended down against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0636 to HK$1.0 from 1.0630. \par \par \uc2  ((Charlie Zhu, Beijing Newsroom +8610 6586-5566 ext 202, Fax +8610 8527-5258 beijing.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw2p06ecd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
