\par\par \b \uc2  HK$ System Vulnerable To China Devaluation - Economist \b0\par\par\uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1999\par 07:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Lacking an orthodoxy to make it solid, Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system is more vulnerable than ever to a devaluation of the Chinese yuan, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board specialist Kurt Schuler says. \par \par \uc2  In a speech planned for a Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  conference Oct. 9, Schuler points out a number of factors he believes make the Hong Kong dollar ripe for speculative pressure. \par \par \uc2  Schuler, a senior economist with the Joint Economic Committee of the U.S. Congress, said his comments are personal views. He provided Dow Jones Newswires with an advance copy of his remarks. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong has a linked exchange rate that officially values the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at HK$7.800 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  But Schuler says he doesn't consider the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  a textbook example of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, which he believes undermines the kind of stability that comes with a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. \par \par \uc2  Schuler says Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system faces two problems: concern that the Hong Kong dollar will be devalued in the long run and the deviations of the market exchange rate, currently around HK$7.7666, from the official rate. \par \par \uc2  Schuler, who believes the HKMA has too much discretion over interest and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, says the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board should be made independent and even given the option to dollarize as part of its defense "armory." \par \par \uc2  He also rejects assertions that Hong Kong's recession is solely the result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, which most economists and the HKMA say has kept real interest rates high in exchange for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability. \par \par \uc2  HKMA Chief Executive Joseph Yam Chi-kwong says in the current edition of his Web site column that Hong Kong's economic adjustment "is a long and painful process" because of the fixed-exchange system, but the ultimate outcome under a floating rate would be less predictable. \par \par \uc2  Schuler counters that if Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system were structured by the book, interest rates wouldn't have to be so high. \par \par \uc2  The economic conditions are mostly the result of broader effects on the region from Japan's "stuttering" economy and "the danger of slow growth in China." he says. "They would affect Hong Kong under any type of monetary system." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par             Says System Not Stronger Now\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Schuler also rejects claims by the HKMA and many other economists that\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board was significantly strengthened a year ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bringing it closer to orthodoxy, the HKMA instituted some changes in\par September, 1998, including the pledge to exchange U.S. dollars for Hong\par Kong dollars from any bank, in an effort to counter the kind of heavy\par market pressure that prompted the government to fight speculators by\par buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as well as stocks and stock futures, in August, 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Hong Kong dollar withstood the Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis where other\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  succumbed, but it remains vulnerable to speculative attacks\par because the HKMA mixes \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  elements,"\par Schuler says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For example, he says, the HKMA's lender-of-last-resort functions\par undermine the financial system. Big foreign banks can rely on their\par overseas \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if they get into trouble, which means the HKMA is\par merely offering insurance to small, weak local banks, he says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Schuler also says, "Another speculative attack against the Hong Kong\par dollar seems likely when China devalues in the coming months."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He notes that China devalued the yuan by 33% in 1994 without creating\par any speculation that Hong Kong would follow. But that was before Hong\par Kong's sovereignty had switched to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 1997 and before last year's\par stock-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sent a signal to the markets that the government had\par "extended its responsibilities from supporting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par supporting the stock market," Schuler says.\par    By James T. Areddy; james.areddy@dowjones.com; 852.9424.2486\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 24-09-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0515GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9o06yot\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
